  "id": "Compound::DB01214 - Gene::153",
  "verbalization": "Metipranolol is implicated in the regulation of Adrb1 through a series of molecular relationships that converge on the beta-adrenergic receptor antagonist class. The evidence suggests that Metipranolol, through its resemblance to other compounds like Oxprenolol and Alprenolol, shares a common pathway with these drugs in binding to Adrb1. This is supported by the observed interactions between Metipranolol and Practolol, which in turn binds to Adrb1, indicating a potential mechanism of action involving receptor modulation. The low common ancestors of Metipranolol with these compounds suggest a shared structural or functional profile, reinforcing the possibility of a mechanistic link. The evidence is consistent with a role in beta-adrenergic receptor antagonism, though the exact nature of the interaction remains to be fully elucidated. The presence of multiple pathways converging on Adrb1 underscores the complexity of the molecular network, but the core mechanism remains plausible.",
